course: history
lesson: 49
title: The Orange Revolution, the Maidan and the war
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: In 2004 a presidential election was falsified, and hundreds of thousands of people in orange scarves stood peacefully on Kyiv's Maidan, the main square, until the Supreme Court ordered a fair re-vote; it was like a class that saw the votes for class captain counted wrongly and calmly asked for a recount.
explain: In late 2013 President Yanukovych dropped an agreement with the EU, students protested, police beat them, and the protest grew into the Revolution of Dignity; in February 2014 about a hundred protesters were killed, remembered as the Heavenly Hundred, and Yanukovych fled to Russia.
explain: Soon Russian troops without badges seized Crimea, and a Russian-backed war began in parts of Donetsk and Luhansk; in July 2014 a missile shot down flight MH17 and all 298 people on board died, and international investigators found the missile was a Buk brought from Russia.
explain: On 24 February 2022 Russia launched a full-scale invasion; Kyiv held, Ukraine freed Kharkiv region and Kherson, and the war goes on, with millions of refugees; Danylo's grandmother came from Kharkiv, and the teacher first asked how his family was.
explain: The wise lesson: history that is still happening needs care, true numbers and kind words; say what most of the world and the courts say, name terrible things truthfully without detail, and remember that maps tell the truth more calmly than slogans.
fact: After a falsified presidential election (one whose votes were counted dishonestly) in November 2004, hundreds of thousands protested peacefully on Kyiv's Maidan; the Supreme Court ordered a re-run, which Viktor Yushchenko won.
fact: Euromaidan began in November 2013 after President Yanukovych dropped an agreement with the EU; after police beat students it grew into a movement against corruption and for dignity, called the Revolution of Dignity.
fact: In February 2014 about a hundred protesters were killed on and near the Maidan, many by snipers (hidden shooters), and police officers also died; the dead protesters are honoured as the «Heavenly Hundred», and Yanukovych fled to Russia.
fact: In February–March 2014 Russian troops without insignia (badges) seized Crimea; a referendum held under occupation was followed by annexation (Russia declaring the land its own), the UN General Assembly called the referendum invalid, and Russia later admitted the soldiers were its troops.
fact: From April 2014 armed groups backed, armed and in key battles reinforced by Russia took parts of the Donetsk and Luhansk regions; some fighters were local, while many weapons and commanders came from Russia.
fact: On 17 July 2014 Malaysia Airlines flight MH17 was shot down over eastern Ukraine and all 298 people died; a Dutch-led (led by the Netherlands) international investigation found a Buk missile brought from Russia, and a Dutch court convicted three men in 2022.
fact: On 24 February 2022 Russia launched a full-scale invasion of Ukraine; its forces reached Kyiv's outskirts but withdrew from the north by early April, and the war continues with heavy losses and millions of refugees, as is widely reported.
fact: Many analysts expected Kyiv to fall within days in 2022; President Zelensky stayed in the capital and Ukrainian forces stopped the attack north of the city.
fact: After Russian troops withdrew from Bucha near Kyiv in spring 2022, investigators found many civilians killed; the UN and international courts are investigating war crimes there.
fact: In autumn 2022 Ukraine liberated much of the Kharkiv region and, in November, the city of Kherson on the Dnipro; large areas in the east and south, and Crimea, remain occupied.
fact: Ukraine is one of the world's biggest exporters of wheat, corn and sunflower oil; when its Black Sea ports were blocked in 2022, food prices rose in many countries, including in Africa and the Middle East.
exercise: Why did the Supreme Court order a new vote in 2004? | The presidential election had been falsified | The ballots were lost at sea on the way to Kyiv | Nobody had voted at all | The candidates had all resigned
exercise: Who won the re-run election of 2004? | Viktor Yushchenko | Viktor Yanukovych | Volodymyr Zelensky | Leonid Kravchuk
exercise: What colour was the symbol of the 2004 protests? | Orange | Bright green | Deep purple | Black and red
exercise: What started the Euromaidan protests in November 2013? | Yanukovych dropping an agreement with the EU | A football match result | A sudden rise in the price of bread and milk only | The Chornobyl accident
exercise: Why is it called the Revolution of Dignity? | After police beat students, people stood up for their rights and honest government | Because it was led by kings | Because it was a protest against a new tax on dignity that the government wanted to bring in | Because it happened at a palace ball
exercise: Who are the Heavenly Hundred? | About a hundred protesters killed in February 2014 | A hundred Cossack hetmans buried together at the old Sich | A famous choir | A hundred angels in an old legend from Kyiv
exercise: What happened to Yanukovych after the shootings? | He fled to Russia | He won the next election | He became mayor of Kyiv | He joined the protesters
exercise: How did most countries see the 2014 Crimea referendum? | As invalid, held under occupation | As a fair and free vote | As a Ukrainian national holiday | As a Eurovision vote by text message
exercise: Who were the soldiers without badges in Crimea in 2014? | Russian troops, as Russia later admitted | Swedish tourists | Ukrainian students who had dressed up as soldiers | UN peacekeepers
exercise: Which two regions saw a Russian-backed war from 2014? | Donetsk and Luhansk | Lviv and Ivano-Frankivsk | Kyiv and Chernihiv | Odesa and Kherson
exercise: How many people died on flight MH17? | 298 | 31 | 33,771 | 100
exercise: Which country led the investigation into MH17? | The Netherlands | Russia's own police | Brazil and Argentina | The People's Republic of China
exercise: When did Russia launch the full-scale invasion? | 24 February 2022 | 24 August 1991 | 26 April 1986 | 22 January 1918
exercise: What did President Zelensky do as Russian forces neared Kyiv? | He stayed in the capital | He fled to Brazil | He hid in Chornobyl | He moved the capital to Lviv
exercise: Who investigates war crimes like those in Bucha? | The UN and international courts | The judges of the Eurovision Song Contest | The referees of the World Cup football final | Nobody at all, because war has no rules
exercise: Which city on the Dnipro did Ukraine free in November 2022? | Kherson | Lviv in the far west | Donetsk in the east | Sevastopol
exercise: Why did food prices rise in Africa when Ukraine's ports were blocked? | Ukraine is a major exporter of grain and oil | Africa buys only Ukrainian cars and tractors | Ukraine grows all the world's rice | The ports sold only toys
test: Danylo says the Orange Revolution was the biggest protest on Earth. Better way to put it? | A big, peaceful protest that won a fair re-vote | The biggest war in history, with millions of soldiers | A tiny protest of ten people | A protest that changed nothing
test: Danylo says the Maidan was about the EU and nothing else. What's the fuller story? | It began over the EU, then grew into a fight for dignity and honest government | Only about football | It was never about the EU at all, but only about the high price of bread in the shops | Only about bread prices
test: «govt shot thousands on the maidan», Danylo says. What's the respectful fix? | About a hundred protesters were killed; the true number honours them | Exactly ten thousand people died in a single afternoon in the square | Nobody was hurt | Only police died
test: Danylo says Russia grabbed Crimea in one night. What's more exact? | It took weeks, with unmarked Russian soldiers | It took a hundred years | Crimea voted freely and fairly with UN observers watching every polling station | Crimea was never taken
test: «donbas rebels were ALL russian soldiers lol». What's fairer? | A Russian-backed war with both local and Russian fighters | All of them were Ukrainian army | All of them were tourists | There was no fighting in Donbas
test: Danylo says a Russian missile shot down a plane full of people in 2014. Is he right? | Yes, that matches the international investigation of MH17 | No, it was brought down by a sudden storm over the steppe | No, nobody was on board | No, it crashed in Brazil
test: Danylo says Russia attacked all of Ukraine in 2022 and Ukraine stood. What's fair to add? | Kyiv held, but much of the south and east is still occupied | Russia never attacked | The war ended in a week, when Russia turned around and went home | Ukraine lost every city
test: «Everyone said Kyiv falls in 3 days». What's the lesson from 2022? | Experts can be wrong; facts on the ground decide | Experts are always right | Kyiv did fall in 3 days | Nobody ever made predictions
test: A younger kid asks what happened in Bucha. What's a good, calm answer? | Many civilians were killed there, and courts are investigating | Tell every terrible detail you saw online, so they know everything | Say nothing ever happened | Make a joke about it
test: Danylo says Ukraine took back everything in 2022. teacher shows a map. What's on it? | Kharkiv region and Kherson freed, large areas still occupied | All of Ukraine and Crimea freed | Nothing freed at all | Ukraine took Moscow
test: Danylo says Ukraine feeds the world. What's more exact? | It is one of the world's biggest grain and oil exporters | It grows every meal on Earth | It imports all its food | It only grows sunflowers for decoration
test: If Ukraine's grain ports stayed blocked for years, what would most likely happen? | Bread and oil would cost more in many countries | Food would get cheaper everywhere | Nothing would change anywhere | Only Ukraine would notice
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the events on the Maidan in February two thousand and fourteen? | Approximately one hundred protesters were killed and the president subsequently fled to Russia | Several thousand protesters were killed in a single hour by soldiers of the regular army | No person was harmed and the president was re-elected | The protests concerned exclusively the price of bread
exam: Identify the conclusion of the international investigation concerning the destruction of flight MH17 on the seventeenth of July, two thousand and fourteen. | The aircraft was struck by a Buk missile brought from Russia | The aircraft was destroyed by a storm | The aircraft landed safely in Kuala Lumpur | The aircraft was struck by a meteorite
exam: Select the response consistent with the position of the United Nations General Assembly in respect of the referendum held in Crimea in two thousand and fourteen. | The referendum was declared invalid | The referendum was declared fair and binding | The referendum was conducted by the Assembly itself | No position was ever taken by any body
exam: Ascertain which statement concerning the full-scale invasion commenced on the twenty-fourth of February, two thousand and twenty-two, is consistent with widely reported facts. | Russian forces withdrew from the north of Kyiv by early April, while the war continued | The capital Kyiv was captured within three days and the lawful government was removed from office | The conflict concluded within one week | No refugees left Ukraine
